solutions.explanation:
First combine the two multiples of \(x\) using a common denominator:
\[
\frac{2x}{3}-\frac{x}{6}
=\frac{4x-x}{6}
=\frac{3x}{6}
=\frac{x}{2}.
\]
If this value is an integer \(n\), then \(x/2=n\), so \(x=2n\). Thus \(x\) must be an even integer. Conversely, every even integer makes the expression an integer.

For example, \(x=2\) makes the original expression equal to \(1\). This positive value of \(x\) is not a multiple of \(3\), \(6\), or \(12\), so none of those additional restrictions is required. The answer is \(\boxed{\text{B}}\).
